__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
github_repositories_SYNTHETIC.csv
yeast_feats_*.csv
dgap_high_powered_results.csv
govphys_quadratic_results.csv
yeast_name_orf_map.csv

# ...but the deposited copy of the GovPhys cohort IS committed, on purpose. The
# root-level file above is a scratch output of a live GitHub fetch and stays
# ignored; cohort-audit/data/ holds the artifact produced by the pre-registered
# CI run, which is what makes the N=992 result reproducible from a clean clone.
# Ignoring it was the reason the audit had to report the gap as open.
!cohort-audit/data/govphys_quadratic_results.csv

# NOTE: yeast_interactome_DEG.csv, scer_essential_genes_DEG.csv and
# scer_essential_orfs.txt were previously ignored here. That is exactly why the
# cohort audit reported "no essentiality labels are committed anywhere" — the files
# existed on the author's disk but never entered the repository, so the outcome
# coupling was not offline-reproducible. They are now COMMITTED under data/yeast/
# (404 KB total). Do not re-ignore them: they are the evidence, not build output.
!data/yeast/yeast_interactome_DEG.csv
!data/yeast/scer_essential_genes_DEG.csv
!data/yeast/scer_essential_orfs.txt

# build artifact: generated by scripts/gen_deep_live.mjs at Vercel build time
public/deep_live_results.json
scratchpad/

# Secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# keel: the single-file executable contains a whole copy of node (~124 MB), so
# it is BUILT, not committed. python3 keel/build_exe.py --binary makes it, and
# keel/test_exe.py proves the 34 KB bundle inside it is the tested one.
keel/dist/keel.blob
keel/dist/keel-*
keel/dist/*.exe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated run-logs that churn on EVERY reproduce run. Listed ONE BY ONE, and
# deliberately NOT as a blanket `results*.json`.
#
# A blanket pattern was the first draft and it is wrong, for the reason this
# file already records two sections above: most results*.json are not build
# output. Measured -- deleting all 69 tracked ones and running the full
# pipeline gives 93/100, because seven suites read a committed copy they never
# rebuild. smi/test_smi.py is the clearest: it opens results_smi.json and
# asserts the run recorded "INVARIANT (BY CONSTRUCTION)", and the pipeline
# never invokes smi/run_smi.py. That file is the thing under audit. Ignoring
# it would delete the evidence, exactly as ignoring the yeast labels did.
#
# These eight are different: each is regenerated by the pipeline itself
# (confirmed -- deleted, then present again after a run), and each changes on
# every run for a reason that is not a finding:
#   * generated_at wall-clock stamps
#   * echo-ledger Merkle roots, which are run-IDs by design -- echo.mjs put()
#     hashes `ts` inside the attested body, so the root MUST differ per run.
#     See echo/echo.test.mjs section F and declarations.md 11.
#   * files_scanned, which counts a live tree and moves when the repo grows
#
# NOT ignored, on purpose: cohort-audit/results_gapclosure.json. It is
# byte-deterministic across runs (0 changed lines), and cohort-audit/
# test_cohort_audit.py reads it at lines 48 and 75 while running BEFORE the
# suite that generates it. Being committed is what hides that ordering
# dependency; untracking it would break the first run on a clean clone. See
# declarations.md 14.
benchmarks-governance/results.json
ei-tests/results.json
governance-os/results_os.json
hf-cohort/results_hf.json
hf-media/results_media.json
hinton-test/results.json
russell-test/results.json
validation-stages/results_stage12.json
